%I A399806 #26 Sep 29 2026 19:57:13
%S A399806 9,10,13,14,18,19,21,23,31,34,35,36,37,42,44,45,46,47,48,50,54,56,61,
%T A399806 62,64,65,67,68,70,73,74,75,77,78,79,82,83,85,86,87,90,92,93,94,97,99,
%U A399806 100,101,103,104,105,106,109,110,112,113
%N A399806 Numbers r such that r = (m-1)/ord_m(2) for some base-2 Fermat pseudoprime m.
%C A399806 Equivalently, the distinct values occurring in A300101, sorted into increasing order.
%C A399806 In particular, no value of A300101 is less than 9.
%C A399806 Let m = Product_{i=1..k} (2*a_i+1) be a base-2 Fermat pseudoprime of rank r = (m-1)/ord_m(2). For r <= 100, m is squarefree: a repeated prime factor would imply a base-2 Wieferich prime p with r >= p+1, while the least such prime is 1093 (A001220).
%C A399806 Put A = Product a_i, L = lcm(a_1,...,a_k), H = A/L, t = lambda(m)/ord_m(2), and u = H*t. Since lambda(m)=2*L, u*(Product(2*a_i+1)-1) = 2*r*Product(a_i).
%C A399806 Moreover r > 2^(k-1)*u, so only finitely many pairs (k,u) are possible for each fixed r.
%C A399806 If all but the final two variables b,c are fixed, the rank equation can be rewritten as (C*b-Q)*(C*c-Q) = Q^2 + C*S, where C=2*(r*B-2*u*P), Q=2*u*P, S=u*(P-1), and P,B are the corresponding products over the fixed prefix.
%C A399806 Thus only finitely many divisor pairs need be checked.
%C A399806 The t=1 branches reduce to Carmichael numbers of fixed index r=(m-1)/lambda(m).
%C A399806 The difficult r=98, u=3, H=1, t=3 branch was completed separately by a divisor-in-residue-class search; all generated cases were exhausted with no candidate.
%H A399806 Richard G. E. Pinch, <a href="https://page.math.tu-berlin.de/~kant/ants/Poster/Pinch_Poster2.pdf">Carmichael numbers with small index</a>, ANTS VII, 2006.
%e A399806 For m=30889, ord_m(2)=3432, so (30889-1)/3432=9.
%e A399806 For m=6601, ord_m(2)=660, so (6601-1)/660=10.
%e A399806 For m=561, ord_m(2)=40, so (561-1)/40=14.
%e A399806 For m=341, ord_m(2)=10, so (341-1)/10=34.
%e A399806 For m=91001, ord_m(2)=2600, so (91001-1)/2600=35.
%e A399806 For m=8885251441, ord_m(2)=246812540, so (8885251441-1)/246812540=36.
%e A399806 For m=4169867689, ord_m(2)=77219772, so (4169867689-1)/77219772=54.
%e A399806 For m=520178982961, ord_m(2)=5654119380, so (520178982961-1)/5654119380=92.
%e A399806 For m=12199433581, ord_m(2)=143522748, so (12199433581-1)/143522748=85.
%e A399806 For m=3679201, ord_m(2)=36792, so (3679201-1)/36792=100.
%Y A399806 Cf. A300101 (the same ranks in pseudoprime order, with repetitions), A001567 (base-2 Fermat pseudoprimes), A306413, A001220 (base-2 Wieferich primes), A174590 (indices of Carmichael numbers).
